// FIXTURE — must be caught by PINE060
//
// No compile error. No warning. The script loads and draws, and the drawing
// is wrong.
//
// Pine divides two integers as an INTEGER, so `rows / affordable` is 2 when
// rows=30 and affordable=14, not 2.142. math.ceil() then rounds a number that
// has no fraction left, the stride comes out one short, and the profile needs
// more boxes than it was granted — so the loop drawing it breaks early and
// the TOP of the profile is never drawn.
//
// Measured with the fault present in this repo: 94.1% of the price span
// covered at 100 rows, 97.0% at 500. Reported from a chart three times. It was
// invisible offline as well, because the interpreter divided the way
// arithmetic does rather than the way Pine does.
//@version=6
indicator("Integer Division", overlay=true)

int rows = 30
int affordable = 14

// bad — ceil() of a value that is already an integer
int stride = int(math.ceil(rows / affordable))

// good — one side forced to float first
int correct = int(math.ceil(rows * 1.0 / affordable))

plot(stride, title="Stride")
plot(correct, title="Correct")
